 transformation uses the original shift-loop and does not change data structures.\n\nSo none of the three archived transformations introduced SoA storage (xs/ys arrays). That was likely from another experiment not in this integration set. Therefore, to integrate only these three, we should keep Group struct with Point *points array as in baseline? But the current source has xs/ys; if we change it back, that might break other experiments? The instructions say \"preserve other experiments and unrelated code\". However, we are producing a new candidate for this integration experiment. We can modify src/knn.c and src/knn.h as needed, but must not affect other files.\n\nGiven the goal is to integrate A,B,C, I think it's safe to revert Group struct back to using Point *points (as in baseline) because that is what those transformations assume. But we also need to incorporate B's static inline distance function and max-heap, and C's OpenMP parallelization with per-thread local top-k arrays.\n\nBut note: A already uses a max-heap; B also uses a max-heap but different implementation. We should choose one consistent approach. Since both are similar, we can adopt the cleaner heap from B (with HeapNode struct) or A's static functions. I'll use B's approach because it's more encapsulated.\n\nFor C: OpenMP parallelization with per-thread local top-k arrays using shift-loop? But if we already have a max-heap for global selection, we could instead have each thread maintain its own max-heap of size k and then merge globally. That would be consistent with both A/B (heap) and C (parallel). The previous successful integration used per-thread heaps.\n\nThus the plan: \n- Keep Group struct as in baseline (Point *points array, not xs/ys).\n- Use static inline euclidean_distance_no_sqrt from B.\n- Implement max-heap with HeapNode struct (1-indexed) for top-k selection.\n- Parallelize over groups using OpenMP with per-thread heaps of size k+1.\n- After parallel region, merge all thread-local heaps into a global heap.\n- Then extract labels and find most frequent.\n\nWe must ensure correctness: the merging order should not affect result because we are selecting top-k globally. The previous failed attempt had a bug; we need to avoid that. In the successful experiment (85d35684357344d0), they used per-thread heaps with NEON SIMD and merged correctly. We'll replicate similar logic but without NEON.\n\nAlso, we must include #include <omp.h> and compile with -fopenmp.\n\nNow produce new src/knn.c and src/knn.h files.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\nknn.h: \n- Include stdio.h, stdlib.h.\n- Define Point struct (x,y floats).\n- Define Group struct with label, length, points pointer.\n- Declare parse functions and euclidean_distance_no_sqrt as static inline? B made it static inline. We'll do that.\n\nknn.c:\n- Include knn.h, omp.h, float.h.\n- Define HeapNode struct (dist, label).\n- Implement heap_swap, sift_down, insert_or_replace similar to current but without NEON.\n- In knn function: get max_threads, allocate per-thread heaps and sizes arrays. Initialize each heap with sentinel values (FLT_MAX dist, -1 label). Use calloc for thread_sizes.\n- Parallel for over groups: for each group, iterate points using euclidean_distance_no_sqrt, insert into local heap.\n- After parallel, merge all per-thread heaps into global heap.\n- Extract labels from global heap (first k elements) into a plain array.\n- Count frequencies and find most frequent label.\n- Free memory.\n\nWe must ensure that the merging step correctly handles cases where some threads have fewer than k points. The insert_or_replace function will handle it because we start with sentinel values; when local_size < k, insertion adds to heap; after reaching k, replacement only if new distance is less than root (max). So global merge should work.\n\nAlso need to include #include <omp.h> and compile accordingly.\n\nNow produce the JSON response. The hypothesis: \"Integrating max-heap top-k selection from A/B with OpenMP parallelization over groups from C will yield a fast correct program by combining efficient streaming heap per thread and deterministic merging, without flattening arrays.\"\n\nTransformations list: [\"Removed flattening of all points into separate x, y, label arrays; instead iterate over groups and compute distances directly from Group structures\", \"Replace inner shift-loop (O(k)) for maintaining sorted nearest neighbors with a max-heap (priority queue) of size k.\", \"Change euclidean_distance_no_sqrt function to static inline to encourage inlining and vectorization.\", \"OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling on groups\", \"Per-thread local top-k reduction using private arrays\", \"Deterministic merge of thread-local lists in global order\"]\n\nBut we need to be precise: The transformations retained from each source. From A: first two items (removed flattening, eliminated malloc calls). Actually A's diff shows it removed flattening and used heap directly; but the transformation description says \"Removed flattening... instead iterate over groups and compute distances directly from Group structures\" and \"Eliminated three malloc calls for flattened arrays\". We'll include those.\n\nFrom B: \"Replace inner shift-loop with max-heap\", \"Change euclidean_distance_no_sqrt to static inline\".\n\nFrom C: \"OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling on groups\", \"Per-thread local top-k reduction using private arrays\", \"Deterministic merge of thread-local lists in global order\".\n\nWe'll list all these as transformations.\n\nsources_kept must be array of IDs from integration_source_ids. We are keeping all three: [\"a379bd55ef8344db\", \"dfb84433121944fa\", \"dbe95ff53cc34a0d\"].\n\nNow write the files content.\n\nLet's produce src/knn.h:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\ntypedef struct {\n    float x;\n    float y;\n} Point;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    char label;\n    int length;\n    Point *points;\n} Group;\n\nvoid on_error() {\n    printf(\"Invalid input file.\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\n    int n;\n    if (scanf(\" n_groups=%d \", &n) != 1) on_error();\n    return n;\n}\n\nPoint parse_point() {\n    float x, y;\n    if (scanf(\" (%f ,%f) \", &x, &y) != 2) on_error();\n    Point point;\n    point.x = x;\n    point.y = y;\n    return point;\n}\n\nGroup parse_next_group() {\n    char label; \n    int length;\n\n    if (scanf(\" label=%c \", &label) != 1) on_error();\n    if (scanf(\" length=%d \", &length) != 1) on_error();\n\n    Group group;\n    group.label = label;\n    group.length = length;\n    group.points = (Point *)malloc(sizeof(Point) * length);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {\n        group.points[i] = parse_point();\n    }\n\n    return group;\n}\n\nint parse_k() {\n    int k;\n    if (scanf(\" k=%d \", &k) != 1) on_error();\n    return k;\n}\n\nstatic inline float euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(Point a, Point b) {\n    float dx = b.x - a.x;\n    float dy = b.y - a.y;\n    return dx * dx + dy * dy;\n}\n```\n\nNote: We changed Group to use points array (not xs/ys). Also made distance function static inline.\n\nNow src/knn.c:\n\n```c\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <float.h>\n\n// Max-heap node structure (1-indexed)\ntypedef struct {\n    float dist;\n    char label;\n} HeapNode;\n\nstatic inline void heap_swap(HeapNode *a, HeapNode *b) {\n    HeapNode t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t;\n}\n\nstatic inline void sift_down(HeapNode *heap, int heap_size, int idx) {\n    while (1) {\n        int largest = idx;\n        int left = 2 * idx;\n        int right = 2 * idx + 1;\n        if (left <= heap_size && heap[left].dist > heap[largest].dist)\n            largest = left;\n        if (right <= heap_size && heap[right].dist > heap[largest].dist)\n            largest = right;\n        if (largest == idx) break;\n        heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[largest]);\n        idx = largest;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic inline void insert_or_replace(float d, char label,\n                                     HeapNode *heap, int *size_ptr, int k) {\n    int hs = *size_ptr;\n    if (hs < k) {\n        // Insert at end and bubble up\n        hs++;\n        int idx = hs;\n        heap[idx].dist = d;\n        heap[idx].label = label;\n        while (idx > 1 && heap[idx / 2].dist < heap[idx].dist) {\n            heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[idx / 2]);\n            idx /= 2;\n        }\n    } else if (d < heap[1].dist) {\n        // Replace root\n        heap[1].dist = d;\n        heap[1].label = label;\n        sift_down(heap, k, 1);\n    }\n    *size_ptr = hs;\n}\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group *groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\n\n    // Allocate per-thread heaps and sizes\n    HeapNode **thread_heaps = (HeapNode**) malloc(max_threads * sizeof(HeapNode*));\n    int *thread_sizes = (int*) calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));\n\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        thread_heaps[t] = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\n        // Initialize with sentinel values: large distance and invalid label.\n        for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\n            thread_heaps[t][i].dist = FLT_MAX;\n            thread_heaps[t][i].label = -1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; gi++) {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        HeapNode *local_heap = thread_heaps[tid];\n        int local_size = 0;\n\n        Group g = groups[gi];\n        Point *pts = g.points;\n        int len = g.length;\n        char label = g.label;\n\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) {\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, pts[j]);\n            insert_or_replace(d, label, local_heap, &local_size, k);\n        }\n\n        thread_sizes[tid] = local_size;\n    }\n\n    // Global heap to merge per-thread results\n    HeapNode *global_heap = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\n    for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\n        global_heap[i].dist = FLT_MAX;\n        global_heap[i].label = -1;\n    }\n    int global_size = 0;\n\n    // Merge all per-thread heaps into the global heap\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        HeapNode *local = thread_heaps[t];\n        int sz = thread_sizes[t];\n        if (sz > k) sz = k;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= sz; i++) {\n            insert_or_replace(local[i].dist, local[i].label,\n                              global_heap, &global_size, k);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Extract labels from the final global heap into a plain array\n    char *labels = (char*) malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    int actual_k = (global_size < k) ? global_size : k;\n    for (int i = 0; i < actual_k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = global_heap[i + 1].label;\n    }\n    // Fill remaining with -1 if not enough points\n    for (int i = actual_k; i < k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = -1;\n    }\n\n    free(global_heap);\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        free(thread_heaps[t]);\n    }\n    free(thread_heaps);\n    free(thread_sizes);\n\n    // Find most frequent label using frequency array\n    int freq[256] = {0};\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] >= 0)\n            freq[(unsigned char)labels[i]]++;\n    }\n\n    char best_label = labels[0];\n    int best_count = freq[(unsigned char)best_label];\n    for (int c = 1; c < 256; c++) {\n        if (freq[c] > best_count) {\n            best_count = freq[c];\n            best_label = (char)c;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(labels);\n    return best_label;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n\n    Group *groups = (Group *)malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; ++i) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n}\n```\n\nThis code integrates all three transformations: no flattening (A), max-heap (B with static inline distance), OpenMP parallelization with per-thread heaps and merge (C). It does not include NEON SIMD.\n\nNow produce the JSON. Ensure content strings have proper escaping for newlines as \\n in JSON. We'll write them as multiline strings but must escape backslashes if any; there are none except maybe in string literals? No, we don't have backslash characters in source code that need escaping at JSON level (only double quotes and control chars). So just replace actual newlines with \\n.\n\nWe'll output the files array with path \"src/knn.h\" and \"src/knn.c\".\n\nNotes: Explain why this integration should work: combines efficient heap per thread, avoids flattening memory overhead, uses static inline for distance to encourage inlining, deterministic merge preserves correctness. Also note that we removed NEON SIMD from previous version because it was not part of the archived transformations.\n\nHypothesis: \"Integrating max-heap top-k selection (from A/B) with OpenMP parallelization over groups (from C) and static inline distance function (B), while avoiding flattening of points into separate arrays, will yield a correct and fast program by combining per-thread streaming heaps with deterministic global merge.\"\n\nTransformations list: as above.\n\nsources_kept: all three IDs.\n\nNow produce final JSON."
